From: Armada <armada@armada.dev>
Subject: [PATCH] Input: rsinput: take a UART batch only as far as it fits

rsinput_rx() dropped any serdev callback larger than its 1024-byte
buffer, and dropped the buffered partial frame whenever the new bytes
would not fit beside it, yet returned count both times, telling serdev
every byte had been consumed. A large batch, or a partial frame meeting
a big one, lost reports.

Accept only the space left and return that. The parser drains complete
frames and keeps the incomplete tail, and the tty layer passes the
unconsumed bytes back straight away. The parser always leaves rx_len
below the buffer size, so each call takes at least one byte.

Adapted to our 0791 reassembly: the change is the same, the context
(and the warning it removes) is ours.
---
diff --git a/drivers/input/joystick/rsinput.c b/drivers/input/joystick/rsinput.c
index 44e50e680..41452f3bd 100644
--- a/drivers/input/joystick/rsinput.c
+++ b/drivers/input/joystick/rsinput.c
@@ -386,14 +386,12 @@ static size_t rsinput_rx(struct serdev_device *serdev, const u8 *data, size_t co
     if (!drv || !data || count == 0)
         return count;
 
-    if (count > sizeof(drv->rx_buf)) {
-        dev_warn_ratelimited(&serdev->dev, "RX burst too long: %zu\n", count);
-        drv->rx_len = 0;
-        return count;
-    }
-
-    if (drv->rx_len + count > sizeof(drv->rx_buf))
-        drv->rx_len = 0;
+    /*
+     * Take only what fits and report that; serdev hands back the rest
+     * once the frames below have drained the buffer. The parser always
+     * leaves rx_len below the buffer size, so at least a byte fits.
+     */
+    count = min(count, sizeof(drv->rx_buf) - drv->rx_len);
 
     memcpy(drv->rx_buf + drv->rx_len, data, count);
     drv->rx_len += count;
